From da28bd477cf456000695b95b76a98f3cd454c546 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Codex <codex@localhost>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 10:11:24 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Audio: absorb clock drift without dropping PCM blocks

The host queue for SPU2 output cut about 125 ms whenever its latency passed 250 ms; IOP and host audio clocks drift, so that happened periodically (18003 frames cut in a capture).

The queue moves into PcmQueue (runtime/ps2_pcm_queue.h), without raylib, so it can be tested:
- the host resamples by at most +-2% toward an 83 ms target instead of cutting;
- Catmull-Rom interpolation between frames, which keeps high frequencies while the read position sits between samples (linear interpolation kept 71% of a 12 kHz tone);
- a backlog beyond four times the target (after a stall or a burst) is still cut to the target once, rather than played off at +2% for up to a minute;
- the read phase stays within the next frame when the queue runs dry.

Tests: interpolation endpoints and a 12 kHz tone at a half-sample offset; 30 s at the host rate and 60 s 1% fast play without gaps or cuts and settle near the target; 0.9 s of backlog is cut once; an empty queue is silent until 50 ms are queued.
